{# The loading bar: a thin line under the header while the next page is on
   its way, so a tap is seen to have been taken.

   Shown in browsers and apps, for ordinary page loads and htmx navigation.

   The bar hangs from the header's bottom edge, inside it, so it goes
   wherever the header goes -- pinned, pulled down with a refresh, scrolled
   under the status bar. Fixed to the screen instead, it parted from the
   header as soon as either moved: iOS insets the page under the status bar,
   and a sticky header and a fixed bar do not agree on where the top is. A
   boosted swap puts a new header up mid-trip, and the bar is carried over
   into it where it had got to. It creeps towards the end and never reaches it on its own
   (ds.css, "The loading bar"); what finishes it is the page arriving --
   htmx's swap, or for a page load, the new document -- or, for a trip that
   never lands (a download, a cancelled load), a timeout.

   Clicks and submits start it at the press. In browsers and apps built on WebKit, a
   press that loads a page holds the load back a frame, until the bar is on
   the screen (leave(), below).
   Any page load that is neither
   -- a script's, or back to a page not kept -- is caught as the page is
   about to be left (beforeunload, below). htmx's own trips are only ever
   the first kind.

   Included by toolbar.jinja, so the painter pages have it as well. The
   script is frontend/site/loadingBar.ts; a boosted swap runs the line below
   again, and the document listeners are added once. #}
<script>if (window.oeeeChrome) window.oeeeChrome.loadingBar();</script>
